cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  AntiFl0ck: self-hosted counter-surveillance for the networks
  you control
message: >-
  If you reference AntiFl0ck in research, writing, or another
  project, please cite it using this metadata.
type: software
authors:
  - name: The AntiFl0ck authors and contributors
    website: https://github.com/AetherAI3/AntiFlock
  - given-names: Brandon
    family-names: Barrante
repository-code: https://github.com/AetherAI3/AntiFlock
url: https://github.com/AetherAI3/AntiFlock
abstract: >-
  AntiFl0ck is open-source, self-hosted counter-surveillance
  software for the networks an operator already controls. It maps
  device and network exposure, labels every finding by evidence
  status (detected, verified, reported, inferred, suspected, or
  unknown), evaluates deterministic operator-defined policy, and
  holds sensitive actions when a trusted route disappears —
  recording each decision in a signed, hash-chained local audit
  log. The pre-alpha release ships a fully simulated coffee-shop
  scenario, a policy engine, a secure-action gate with a
  TypeScript SDK, read-only Tailscale and Headscale probes, and
  the Third-Eye dashboard. Production enforcement remains open
  engineering work. AntiFl0ck is an independent project, is not
  affiliated with or endorsed by Flock Safety, and does not
  interfere with third-party surveillance infrastructure.
keywords:
  - counter-surveillance
  - privacy
  - network-security
  - zero-trust
  - self-hosted
  - threat-modeling
  - audit-log
  - opsec
